WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.366 --> 00:00:06.487
[Music]

00:00:07.042 --> 00:00:09.060
[Patricia F. Brennan, PhD] Good&nbsp;
afternoon. I'm Patti Brennan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.900
the director of the National Library of&nbsp;
Medicine. And today I'm excited to have you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:18.251
join me in a conversation with one of our most&nbsp;
distinguished investigators, Dr. Eugene Koonin.

00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:22.530
Today, our conversation is going to&nbsp;
travel through a wide range of ideas from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:29.057
the communication of cells across time to the&nbsp;
comparison of viruses and other cellular life.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.900
Dr. Koonin's thinking about how&nbsp;
various life forms evolve over time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.160
has been instrumental in driving many&nbsp;
discoveries in contemporary biology.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.040
Did you start out experimentally or&nbsp;
computationally to study viruses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:49.271
or did they become a serviceable good&nbsp;
model for you to study evolution?

00:00:49.543 --> 00:00:55.860
[Eugene Koonin, PhD] I started my career as&nbsp;
an experimental virologist trying to study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:03.018
RNA viruses, maybe without outstanding success.  But 

00:01:03.018 --> 00:01:13.620
soon after, these RNA viruses have indeed&nbsp;
become an excellent, probably best at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:20.000
playground for the emerging discipline&nbsp;
of comparative genomics. Because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:27.240
for the simple reasons that, at the time, these were the&nbsp;
complete genomes that could could be sequenced.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.739
[Brennan] What got you into the computational&nbsp;
tools for understanding viruses?

00:01:32.882 --> 00:01:40.740
[Koonin] The first complete genomes of any&nbsp;
biological entities have started to accumulate.&nbsp;

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.660
And these biological entities, at the&nbsp;
time, were exclusively small viruses.

00:01:46.376 --> 00:01:50.152
And these represented both an&nbsp;
opportunity and a challenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.312
An opportunity because these were complete&nbsp;
genomes to compare and try to understand.

00:01:55.312 --> 00:02:01.200
A challenge because, without special&nbsp;
effort, without developing dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:07.334
computational approaches, very little&nbsp;
could be understand from those sequences.&nbsp;

00:02:07.334 --> 00:02:13.200
I don't think that the community, at the time,&nbsp;
was entirely prepared for that situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:20.964
but it was rectified fairly soon, and I am I'm&nbsp;
very pleased to have played some role in that.

00:02:21.126 --> 00:02:25.320
[Brennan] Well, we've talked before about&nbsp;
your interest in genomic evolution and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:30.283
that the the manner in which organisms&nbsp;
change over time. And I'd like you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:33.960
talk a little bit about that now, and if&nbsp;
you could specifically talk a little about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.355
the challenge of understanding&nbsp;
conservation versus mutation.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:48.000
[Koonin] All genomes that exists, exist&nbsp;
on our planet, result from &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:57.180
billions and millions of years of evolution. To put it&nbsp;
very simply, when we compare genomes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.632 --> 00:03:03.360
either closely related, or&nbsp;
distantly related organisms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:11.185
we delineate conserved and less conserved&nbsp;
variable paths. And what we can see is that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.185 --> 00:03:18.720
highly conserved part are involved in functions&nbsp;
that are essential for the organism's survival.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:33.967
Conversely, the parts that are variable&nbsp;
contain genes that are involved in interactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.967 --> 00:03:38.501
between organisms and in interactions&nbsp;
between organisms and the environment.

00:03:38.501 --> 00:03:44.220
[Brennan] So when you speak about conserved, this&nbsp;
is not only sections of the gene that appear over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:50.543
generations of the organism, but also that&nbsp;
appear commonly across groups of organisms.

00:03:50.543 --> 00:03:58.500
[Koonin] It's useful to note that, by now it's a&nbsp;
firmly established plain fact, that all cellular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:07.500
organisms - I'm not talking about viruses - all&nbsp;
cellular organisms that exist on this planet originate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:16.042
from a single common ancestor, a so-called LUCA.
Last Universal Cellular Ancestor that existed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.042 --> 00:04:20.957
about 4 billion years ago.&nbsp;
Just a little bit less maybe.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:26.922
This was probably the most brilliant hypothesis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.922 --> 00:04:31.020
in the history of science proposed&nbsp;
by none other than Charles Darwin.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:38.280
The entire history of life, an unimaginably&nbsp;
long history of life on our planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:47.525
spanning about 4 billion years, is permeated&nbsp;
by persisting evolutionary relationships.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.857 --> 00:04:53.820
There is, to be even a little more specific,&nbsp;
there is about a hundred of genes that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.820 --> 00:05:00.240
shared by all cellular organisms, from&nbsp;
mammals to plants to amoeba to bacteria.

00:05:00.876 --> 00:05:04.084
[Brennan] I'm going to turn our&nbsp;
attention to your experiences here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.084 --> 00:05:08.389
at the National Library of Medicine.&nbsp;
And you've been here 30 years? 

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.003
[Koonin] Actually even a&nbsp;
little over a little longer.

00:05:11.003 --> 00:05:14.820
[Brennan] A little longer.
[Koonin] Exact. Exactly 31 now.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:21.180
[Brennan] 31 years. And you have had an&nbsp;
illustrious career, many trainees and many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:25.687
many, many papers. Wonder if you can talk a&nbsp;
little bit about how the National Library of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.687 --> 00:05:31.080
Medicine's resources have shaped your work&nbsp;
and how you have helped shape our resources.

00:05:31.668 --> 00:05:40.728
[Koonin] It has been an incredibly welcoming&nbsp;
and fertile environment for the evolution of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.728 --> 00:05:53.160
my own scientific career, my my research group.&nbsp;
And combines very good resources with pretty much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.160 --> 00:06:01.543
unlimited academic freedom. Which sounds almost&nbsp;
too good to be true, but nevertheless is true.

00:06:01.543 --> 00:06:06.420
[Brennan] You spoke at one time of the&nbsp;
importance of the BLAST algorithm for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:10.740
your work and then further modifications&nbsp;
of that. Could you speak a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.471
about the analytical tools that you&nbsp;
use and what we provide for that?

00:06:14.543 --> 00:06:23.460
[Koonin] BLAST is a centerpiece of&nbsp;
computational biology and genomics.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:33.420
A completely indispensable tool&nbsp;
which quickly compares sequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:38.040
of nucleic acids and proteins. And their are.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:44.321
At the time of it's development, which&nbsp;
incidentally coincided with my arrival here.&nbsp;

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:51.977
This was by far superior by any other&nbsp;
available methods. It was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.208 --> 00:07:01.020
the only real game in town. The only&nbsp;
way to efficiently compare the gene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:04.631
and genomic sequences. Very timely development.

00:07:04.709 --> 00:07:06.604
I don't think that any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:15.000
tool or method developed here really compares with&nbsp;
BLAST in importance. It should be noted however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:25.937
that there is substantial competition in the world&nbsp;
now that hopefully advances the field as a whole.

00:07:26.084 --> 00:07:28.440
[Brennan] I'd like you to reflect for a moment on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:34.402
the contribution that you delight in&nbsp;
the most in your scientific career.

00:07:34.751 --> 00:07:39.660
[Koonin] Perhaps I should mention the&nbsp;
CRISPR system. And what CRISPR is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:50.640
is widely known because this is known as the new&nbsp;
methodology for genome engineering and editing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:56.400
which is far more efficient and&nbsp;
fast than previously available ones.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:05.938
But, in in biology itself, this is&nbsp;
something also of great interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.938 --> 00:08:14.580
because CRISPR is an adaptive immunity&nbsp;
system in in microbes in in bacteria.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:23.640
And before this was discovered, it was not even,&nbsp;
no one even suspected that adaptive immunity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:29.220
fundamentally similar to our&nbsp;
own, existed in bacteria.

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:35.795
And with my colleagues, through&nbsp;
comparative genomics approaches as usual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:45.125
we have been instrumental in the discovery&nbsp;
and further characterization of this system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:52.042
Many labs contributed, but I think I&nbsp;
think our contribution is noticeable.

00:08:52.042 --> 00:08:55.980
[Brennan] I want to close by congratulating&nbsp;
you on your election to the National Academy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:03.420
of Medicine this year. I was quite proud to have&nbsp;
another member of our NLM staff become recognized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:07.543
so widely for critical contributions,&nbsp;
and I congratulate you personally.

00:09:07.543 --> 00:09:12.258
[Koonin] Thank you so much. It's&nbsp;
a certain certainly a great honor.

00:09:13.024 --> 00:09:17.492
[Music]

00:09:17.492 --> 00:09:19.364
[Music fades to Silence]

